{"componentChunkName":"component---src-templates-blog-post-tsx","path":"/blog/2024-04-20--creating-a-modern-ish-usable-linux-desktop-with-notion/","result":{"data":{"site":{"siteMetadata":{"disqus":"edward-j-schwartz"}},"post":{"htmlAst":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometime while I was in graduate school, I started using the\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://www.notionwm.net"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"notion"}]},{"type":"text","value":" window manager.  (Actually, at the time, I\nthink it was ion3.)  Notion is a tiling window manager that is geared towards\nkeyboard usage instead of mouse usage.  But let's be honest: I've been using\nnotion for so long that I simply prefer it over anything else."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Notion, like most minor window managers, is a bit spartan.  It does not provide\na "},{"type":"element","tagName":"em","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"desktop environment"}]},{"type":"text","value":".  It really just manages windows.  There are some\nfeatures of a desktop environment that I don't need, such as a launcher.  I know\nall the commands that I use; I don't need a GUI to list them for me.  But it's\noften the little things that get you, such as locking the screen, or using the\nmedia keys on your keyboard to adjust the volume.  I used to be (more of) a hardcore nerd\nand relished in my ability to craft a super-complex "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":".xsessionrc"}]},{"type":"text","value":" file with all\nkinds of bells, whistles and utilities connected as if with duct tape.  But as I\ngrow older, sometimes I just want my computer to work."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a long while now, I've found that running notion alongside GNOME for\n\"desktop stuff\" to work pretty well.  For a long time, I followed an "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://web.archive.org/web/20160205182002/http://awesome.naquadah.org:80/wiki/Quickly_Setting_up_Awesome_with_Gnome"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"old Wiki\npost"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nabout how to combine GNOME with Awesome WM.  This worked really welll with\nGNOME 2."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many people say that you can't use GNOME 3 with window managers other than GNOME\nShell.  I've actually had pretty good luck copying the Ubuntu gnome-session and\nreplacing Gnome Shell with notion.  The above Awesome WM Wiki also shows how to\ndo it.  Unfortunately, I've found that some features do not work, such as the\nkeyboard media keys, much to my dismay.  Do media keys matter that much?  Yes,\nyes, they do. This apparently broke when "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://askubuntu.com/a/906596"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"GNOME Shell started binding the media\nkeys"}]},{"type":"text","value":" instead of "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"gnome-settings-daemon"}]},{"type":"text","value":".  There\nused to be a "},{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{"className":["language-text"]},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"gnome-fallback-media-keys-helper"}]},{"type":"text","value":" utility around that would\nsimulate this behavior, but it seems to have disappeared."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As I was trying to fix this problem, I came across a "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://zork.net/~st/jottings/gnome-i3.html"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"blog\npost"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and an unrelated but similar\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/nmakel/i3-gnome"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"github repo"}]},{"type":"text","value":" both describing how to use the\ni3 window manager with GNOME.  TLDR: GNOME Flashback is a still supported\nvariant of GNOME that is explicitly designed to support third-party window\nmanagers.  Whereas GNOME Shell incorporates both the window manager and other\nstuff such as handling media keys, GNOME Flashback has the \"other stuff\" in a\nseparate component that is designed to be used with a window manager such as\nmetacity.  But it works just fine with notion!  Best of all, both my media keys\nand screen locking work.  Hurray!"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"p","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because I hate setting up stuff like this, I've actually been hard at work\npackaging up my Linux computer configuration into reusable ansible components.\nIt takes a little longer than doing it manually of course, but it's not too bad\nand it's pretty easy to read. I'm making my "},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/ansible-notion"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"notion\nrole"}]},{"type":"text","value":" available here in case anyone\nwants to try out my setup.  Most of the logic is\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"a","properties":{"href":"https://github.com/edmcman/ansible-notion/blob/main/tasks/main.yml"},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"here"}]},{"type":"text","value":" if\nyou are curious what is involved.  Below are a few snippets to show how Ansible\nmakes it relatively easy to manipulate configuration files."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\n"},{"type":"element","tagName":"div","properties":{"className":["gatsby-highlight"],"dataLanguage":"yaml"},"children":[{"type":"element"